Darwin: where panfrost's processor time goes, in a process that draws through Metal.

A34 (local to this port; nothing here goes upstream). Counters and timers in panfrost's draw
path, batch handling and buffer allocation, kept only when redwood_pan_metal is set (air_metal.c
sets it when it opens its device); a process that only uses OpenGL ES times and counts nothing.
The slots are named in src/compiler/air/redwood_pan_prof.h (userland/mesa/air), read and
cleared by air_metal.c (rwa_pan_profile) for the plug-in's camprof ("rwm pan" lines):

- the parts of a draw: prepare_draw, the vertex count and index buffer, update_state_3d
  (depth/stencil, blend, vertex), update_shader_state (textures, samplers, shader and varying
  descriptors, uniform buffers and push constants, images and storage buffers), launch_draw;
  draws that found no current batch (all state dirty);
- batches begun, pools' new backing buffers, panfrost_bo_create and those of them not from
  Mesa's cache, batches submitted from inside a draw for an access to what they use;
- batches submitted with work and empty, and why each with work was submitted (panfrost's own
  reason strings, set around the submission by whoever asked for it).
- once, at the first batch: what 4 KB of stores, a memcpy and loads cost in a batch pool's
  memory against malloc's (redwood_pan_memtest) -- every descriptor of a draw is written there.

One change of behaviour, in a Metal process only: panfrost_flush_all_batches submits the batches
there are, and no longer begins the compute batch and the current framebuffer's render batch
when there are none only to submit them empty (two of the three batches of every flush on a pass
switch were such in boot 68).

